# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows converts to CRLF on checkout by default, which is fine for
# Kotlin and XML and fatal for anything with a shebang: a shell script with
# CRLF endings has an interpreter line of "#!/bin/sh\r", the kernel cannot
# resolve it, and the error is "not found" for a file that is plainly there.
#
# That bit the hub's first container build — `gradlew` had been copied from a
# Windows working tree, and the Docker build failed at step 7 with exit 127.
# The Dockerfile now strips CR defensively as well; this stops the file from
# acquiring them in the first place.
#
# LF EVERYWHERE, and not as a matter of taste. `* text=auto` left 334 of the
# 447 tracked files CRLF in a Windows working tree, and scripts/check.sh reads
# most of them: a pattern anchored with `$`, an `awk` range that ends on `^}$`,
# a `case` on a `"…"\n` fragment — all of them match a line that now ends in a
# carriage return they were never written to expect. Eight guards failed OPEN
# that way, reporting clean on a tree they could no longer see into, and the
# gate cannot report that it has gone blind. The blobs have always been LF
# (checked: not one carries a CR), so this changes nothing in history and only
# stops the checkout from rewriting them on the way out.
#
# It cost hours in other ways too. Every session that edited a file after a
# branch switch found its `\n` patterns silently defeated, and `cat -A` on
# this MSYS build does not even show the CR — it took a perl hexdump to see.

*           text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only, and cmd.exe genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# PowerShell does not. It reads LF happily on 5.1 and on 7, and check.ps1 is
# read by the bash gate (guard 10 holds the two mirrors' headings together),
# where CRLF breaks the same patterns as anywhere else.
*.ps1       text eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.apk       binary
*.keystore  binary
*.jar       binary
